A||||3||DaveL||2.5 Block Question||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||07-16-2001||03:35 PM||||I will indeed be scouring the south Florida junkyards for an 87-88 2.5 engine since mine died an untimely death. I understand that any 87 or 88 2.5 from a Fiero will plug right in. According to that Ogre, any 87-91 Olds Calais 2.5, or almost any 87-91 GM 2.5 from a front wheel drive will also fit. After transferring brackets and what not, will I still use the same ECM ?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||07-16-2001||03:41 PM||||If all the sensors are the same, the ECM should be happy.||||reg||1
Z||000002||uechiman||07-16-2001||07:22 PM||||I have an 87' 2.5 with 42,000 miles on it ready to haul away.<BR>send me an e-mail<BR><p>[This message has been edited by uechiman (edited 07-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||07-16-2001||07:36 PM||||All the FWD installs of this motor will work in Fiero once the brackets/pulleys are changed. I've seen it done in both directions... (FWD to Fiero and Fiero to FWD.)<P>Sensors are the same. In most cases the FWD ECM is the same. The PROM is not. You'll need to reuse the Fiero harness and air cleaner for the new block. (The air cleaner is the same except the MAP sensor hole is on the wrong side of FWD ones.)<P>If you use one after the 88 model year check the EGR vacuum plumbing. I'm told some later installs of the DIS motor use an EGR control valve like the V6 does. It' no big deal, you'll just have to put the Fiero EGR setup on it and the Fiero harness.<P>(I have not been able to verify if any car uses the EGR solenoid. I've only actually seen it in an S10 truck. No, I don't think the S10 block will work. I don't think it's got the water pump mount on the side for starters...)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-16-2001).]||||reg||1
